from write_records import add,N,BASE
add('socg',1,'Silence after a proposal has several social positions','Replace a single “quiet members agree or disengage” interpretation with an information-and-authority map that changes the next social move.','In a constructed planning meeting, I initially treated a quiet reviewer as weak evidence of low interest; no access or speaking-opportunity facts had yet been separated.', '''Social setting: a fictional team choosing an exhibition layout. Players: A proposer can change the design; B reviewer sees only a low-resolution image; C coordinator controls the meeting agenda; D installer knows the room dimensions. History: one draft was circulated, and B has not commented. Formal power belongs to C over the decision slot; A has informal ownership; D has unique feasibility information.

Stated layer: “Any objections to layout two?” and silence from B. Unstated candidates remain hypotheses: B agrees; B is uninterested; B cannot inspect the image; B believes C has already decided; B is waiting for D’s dimensions. The known low-resolution access specifically supports incomplete information. No private emotional state is invented.

Perspective models:

| Player | Wants / fear / belief | Sees other roles | Constraint and emotion status |
|---|---|---|---|
| A | Test a distinctive layout / lose design coherence / silence looks encouraging | B as reviewer, C as decider | Needs feedback; feeling unknown |
| B | Evaluate legibility / approve something unseen / image unreadable at available resolution | A owns design; C owns time | Cannot inspect detail; feeling unknown |
| C | End with feasible choice / meeting overruns / participants have draft | A presents; B/D respond | Decision slot limited; feeling unknown |
| D | Build workable layout / unsafe fit / room dimensions constrain options | A’s draft provisional | Needs dimensions considered; feeling unknown |

Reaction comparison: if C declares approval from silence, A treats layout as settled, B has no factual basis to endorse it, and D’s later constraint becomes an apparent reversal. If C first shares readable detail and asks D for the specific dimension, B can produce a reviewable objection or assent. A’s reaction to a late objection would then concern content, not a supposed broken agreement. These are conditional predictions, not observed emotions.

Eight social leverage points: readable common input; explicit distinction between proposal and decision; one invitation per expertise domain; B’s option to withhold judgment; D’s dimension before final choice; A’s ability to name what is artistically non-negotiable; C’s clear decision authority; a correction route after meeting. Highest-impact lever in this case is common access to the proposal because without it B cannot perform the requested review.

Move generated: “Before calling this approved, here is the readable layout. D, which dimension constrains installation? B, please separate what you can assess now from what remains unclear.” Tone is direct and invitational. Sequence: common input, relevant constraint, review, decision. Audience is the existing team; no message is sent. Risks: over-questioning delays a settled decision; mitigation is one specific access check. Fallback: mark B’s review unresolved rather than infer assent.

Certificate: the known unreadable input provides an information explanation that the low-interest hypothesis lacks. It does not prove interest; it defeats using silence alone as evidence for a settled preference. The second-order consequence is concrete: premature approval converts later information into apparent social inconsistency.''','The generated social move now checks participation opportunity and shared information before assigning meaning to silence.','In a distinct later transcript, the revised classifier preserves an absent stakeholder’s unresolved position instead of adding them to a consensus.','KEEP','Content includes four different role perspectives and conditional second-order effects. Organization separates observed access, inferred motive, and decision authority so one cannot substitute for another.','Test silence with confirmed full access; compare private and group invitation effects in an actual case; model a participant who explicitly delegates their vote.',N+' Four perspectives, five silence hypotheses, two action/reaction sequences, eight leverage points, and a later transcript classification.','New constructed transcript: chair says “Everyone agreed yesterday”; member replies “I was not invited to that channel.” Initial consensus summary would list that member as agreeing from absence of objection. Applying the perspective map produces “participants in yesterday’s channel agreed; this member’s position is unresolved.” The actual generated consensus record now fits the explicit participation fact and does not require a human motive guess.')
(BASE/'consolidation-02.md').write_text('''Intended mind change: Reuse the next four kept transformations across motivation and social interpretation without flattening their different targets.

# Four more keeps applied through case triggers

Inputs: 024-funr-1, 025-mp-1, 026-trust-1, 073-socg-1 plus the first consolidation. Starting organization: a general list headed “better interpretations and ordering.” That theme is too broad to recover the operative conditions.

| New task trigger | Kept transformation | Limit | Source |
|---|---|---|---|
| A question already presumes a motive | Specify relation, expectation, and viewpoint | Constructed alternative perspectives do not identify a real motive | 024-funr-1.md |
| An easy task is placed before an opportunity window | Respect readiness and deadline before motivational ordering | Human energy and motivation effects are still untested | 025-mp-1.md |
| A single trust label governs unlike tasks | Separate competence evidence from commitment reliability | No general benevolence judgment follows | 026-trust-1.md |
| Silence is treated as assent or disinterest | Check information and participation opportunity | Full access still does not prove agreement | 073-socg-1.md |

Three organizations compared: alphabetical skill index; outcome-only list; trigger plus exception table. Two later uses were actually answered from each view.

Use A: “A reliable organizer miscalculates one rate; an occasionally late analyst calculates it correctly. Who checks the next rate?” Alphabetical view locates trust eventually but gives no task key. Outcome-only view “calibrate trust” does not choose a dimension. Trigger table returns calculation competence and produces analyst for the rate, organizer for timing. The benevolence limit is preserved.
Use B: “The person did not object to a plan they could not see. Is that approval?” Alphabetical view requires selecting social cognition among several relevant titles. Outcome-only “understand perspectives” leaves approval scope unspecified. Trigger table produces “position unresolved pending accessible input,” retaining participation and access as different requirements.

The trigger table is used to draft the next empathy and listening cases. It also retrieves the first consolidation’s constraint qualifier for any allocation-to-value inference. The detailed case records stay available; a time-sequence query can still use chronology. There is no human search-time measurement.

Actual mind change: Later reliance and assent questions are now routed through their concrete inference trigger, not a broad theme or skill title.

Benefit: The two generated answers select the task-relevant dimension and preserve a material exception; the outcome-only list is insufficient to reconstruct either on its own.

Verdict: KEEP

Organization: Only existing kept content was regrouped. Four new entries preserve distinct causes rather than merging them into “be careful.” No further improvement was found among these views for these uses; general optimality is not claimed.

Next attempts: Test a case that triggers two entries with competing recommendations; compare timeline retrieval; examine whether a short trigger becomes ambiguous in a different domain.
''')
add('socg',2,'A well-meant coalition can change who may disagree','Model the second-order effect of privately securing allies before an open project discussion.','Privately aligning supportive participants seems a practical way to make a meeting productive, but it changes the information and power conditions for people outside those conversations.', '''Constructed community case: A proposes a reading group; B has already privately agreed; C facilitates; D is newly invited and does not know about the prior agreement. A wants lively sessions, B wants a predictable schedule, C wants a workable group, D wants freedom to explore without mandatory preparation. The room is online and text-only; tone, face, and bodily signals are unavailable.

Stated purpose: “choose a format together.” Unstated candidates: A views the meeting as confirmation; B views it as scheduling; C views it as open selection; D views it as a genuine invitation to shape the activity. Only the private A/B agreement is a stipulated fact; other interpretations remain case hypotheses.

Four perspective models: A values momentum and fears endless discussion; B values reliability and fears schedule churn; C values participation and fears unclear decisions; D values inquiry and fears inherited obligations. A can propose, C can structure the meeting, everyone can decline membership. D’s lower history/access gives a different starting point without implying lower competence or less interest.

Action prediction: if A begins “we have agreed on weekly chapters,” B reinforces it, C treats format as settled, and D’s objection becomes a request to undo a group agreement D never joined. Second-order effect: D’s dissent can then be interpreted as lack of commitment rather than an input to the promised format choice. Alternative action: disclose “A and B favor weekly chapters; the group format is still open,” preserving the actual agreement’s scope.

Eight leverage points: disclose prior agreement; distinguish existing members from invitees; separate schedule from content; make preparation optional if genuinely optional; let D describe a desired session; state what cannot change; record open choices before voting; permit joining later. Highest-impact lever: label which decisions are already committed and which are actually open.

Generated move: “Two of us prefer weekly chapters. We have not decided for the group. Let’s compare a chapter meeting, a question-led meeting, and alternating sessions; joining is optional.” Risk: falsely reopening an externally fixed schedule; mitigation: name that constraint separately. Fallback: offer parallel or alternating formats without claiming full consensus.

Later application: a workshop invitation now distinguishes “date fixed” from “agenda open.” This is an executed wording change. It reuses the participation/scope distinction kept in socg-1, so it earns no new KEEP. Real participation and trust effects remain unknown.''','The social model now includes how a preparatory coalition changes the meaning of later dissent; a narrower invitation is generated.','The invitation’s open choices are explicit; no observed human cooperation improvement is established.','UNRESOLVED','Content separates useful preparation from undisclosed precommitment. Organization records decision status before predicting social reaction.','Observe a meeting with disclosed precommitments; vary whether new participants can opt out; compare fixed-time and flexible-content invitations.',N+' Four perspectives, eight leverage points, a second-order reaction chain, two alternative moves, and a later invitation.')
